The metric $\rho$ on $S(\Omega)$ can be defined as
\begin{align*}
\rho(x,y)=\mu\left(\left\{\omega\in \Omega: x(\omega)\neq y(\omega)\right\}\right),\, x, y \in S(\Omega).
\end{align*}

In particular, let $\Omega=\left\{1, \ldots, n\right\}$ be an $n$-point set equipped with the  counting measure $\mu,$ defined by
$\mu(A)=|A|$ for all subsets $A\subseteq \Omega,$ where $|A|$ denotes  the cardinality of $A.$ In this setting, the associated space
$S(\Omega)$ is isomorphic to $\mathbb{C}^n.$
Here, $\nabla=\{0,1\}^n$ represents the set of all binary vectors of length of $n.$ For a non-zero element $a=(a_1, \ldots, a_n)\in \mathbb{C}^n$ its partial  inverse and support  are the following:
$i(a)=(i(a_1), \ldots, i(a_n)),$ where $i(a_k)=\frac{1}{a_k}$ for $a_k\neq 0$ and $i(a_k)=0$ otherwise; $s(a)=(\varepsilon_1, \ldots, \varepsilon_n),$ where $\varepsilon_k=|{\rm sign} a_k|$ for all $k=1, \ldots, n.$ The rank metric $\rho$ on $\mathbb{C}^n$ is defined by
\begin{align*}
\rho(a,b)=\frac{\left|\{k\in \overline{1, n}: a_k\neq b_k\}\right|}{n}=\sum\limits_{k=1}^n \frac{|{\rm sign}(a_k-b_k)|}{n}.
\end{align*}
More generally, if  $\left(\Omega, \Sigma, \mu\right)$ is an atomic measure space with a finite, strictly positive, countable additive measure $\mu,$ then
$S(\Omega)\cong \mathbb{C}^\gamma,$ where $\gamma$ is the cardinality of the set of  atoms in $\nabla.$

(b) Let $\cM$ be a type I$_n$-factor for some integer  $n\ge 1.$
Then $\cM$ is $*$-isomorphic to the algebra $\mathbb{M}_n$ of all $n\times n$ complex matrices. In this case,
the rank metric on $\mathbb{M}_n$ is defined as
$$
\rho(x,y)=\frac{{\rm rank}(x-y)}{n},\, x, y \in \mathbb{M}_n,
$$
where ${\rm rank}(a)$ denotes the rank of the matrix $a.$
\end{example}

