\title{Spin-split magnon bands induce pure spin current in insulating altermagnets}
\author{Sankar Sarkar}
\email{sankars24@iitk.ac.in}
\affiliation{Department of Physics, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur-208016, India}
\author{Amit Agarwal}
\email{amitag@iitk.ac.in}
\affiliation{Department of Physics, Indian Institute of Technology Kanpur, Kanpur-208016, India}

\begin{abstract}
Altermagnets offer a promising platform for dissipationless spin transport by combining zero net magnetization with spontaneous non-relativistic spin-split bands. However, their magnonic transport properties remain largely unexplored. Here, we develop a quantum-kinetic theory for thermally driven magnon currents that cleanly separates Berry-curvature–driven intrinsic contributions from Drude-like scattering-dependent terms. Applying this framework to a collinear honeycomb antiferromagnet with anisotropic next-nearest-neighbor exchange and Dzyaloshinskii–Moriya interaction, we reveal spin-split magnon bands that support both intrinsic and extrinsic spin Nernst and Seebeck currents. For realistic parameters, we predict a sizable magnon spin-splitting angle (about 3.3 degrees) and a pure transverse spin current capable of exerting a strong spin-splitter torque suitable for magnetization switching.
%\textcolor{blue}{
%Altermagnetism, the coexistence of non-relativistic spin-split bands in momentum space and compensated magnetic order in real space, offers a promising platform for generating pure spin currents. While the electronic properties of altermagnets have been extensively studied, their magnonic properties are less explored. To explore the magnon transport properties, we use a density matrix-based quantum kinetic theory framework. Within this framework we calculate the magnonic spin Nernst and Seebeck conductivities under a thermal gradient.We identify an intrinsic contribution to the spin current governed by the Berry curvature, and an extrinsic Drude-like term proportional to the magnon relaxation time. To illustrate these findings, we consider a collinear antiferromagnetic model on a honeycomb lattice incorporating anisotropic next-nearest-neighbor (NNN) exchange couplings and Dzyaloshinskii–Moriya interaction (DMI). These combined interactions result in spin-split magnon bands that carry opposite spin angular momentum. Finally, we demonstrate a tunable magnon spin-splitting mechanism that enables the generation of \textit{pure} spin currents, capable of inducing a sizable spin-splitter torque (SST) relevant for magnetization switching applications.}
 %\textcolor{blue}{and show a significant amount of spin-splitter torque (SST) generated by this pure spin current to an adjacent ferromagnet}, 
%laying the foundation for spintronic devices based on altermagnetic insulators. 
\end{abstract}
