Developing energy-efficient methods for information processing is crucial for advancing technology and understanding the physics of emergent quantum materials. One promising strategy is to utilize spin transport without accompanying charge transport, thereby eliminating energy loss due to Joule heating \cite{Sarma_2004, Bader_2010, hirohata_2020}. In magnetic insulators, spin current can be carried by magnons—the bosonic quasiparticles associated with collective excitations of the magnetic order \cite{Maekawa_2012, blundell_2001, Baltz_2018}. As charge-neutral entities, magnons offer a compelling platform for dissipationless spin transport, which lies at the heart of the growing field of magnon spintronics \cite{Maekawa_2012, chumak_2015, blundell_2001, Baltz_2018}. However, conventional magnetic materials impose significant limitations: in ferromagnets, the spin of the magnon is fixed by its chirality, while in antiferromagnets, spin transport typically relies on relativistic spin–orbit coupling mechanisms, which is weaker than exchange interactions, limiting the efficiency and tunability of the response \cite{Cheng_2016, Vladimir_2016, Rezende_2019,Rezende_2016, Shen_2019, Xiao_2010, Rezende_2014, Uchida_2010}.

A recently proposed class of materials, known as altermagnets (AMs), offers a novel route to overcome these challenges. Altermagnets exhibit macroscopically vanishing magnetization due to compensated sublattices, as in antiferromagnets, yet their band structures show spontaneous spin-splitting in momentum space, similar to ferromagnets \cite{Sinova_2022, Fender_2025, Gomonay_2024, Cui_2023, krempasky_2024, Liu_2024, Jairo_2022}. The spin-split magnon bands in AMs result from anisotropic exchange coupling that stems either from the local crystal environment due to non-magnetic atoms or from the orbital anisotropy of the magnetic ions themselves \cite{Cui_2023, Brekke_2023, Alberto_2024, Lee_2024}. Furthermore, the presence of two magnon modes with opposite spin angular momentum provides a natural platform for encoding and manipulating spin information \cite{Cheng_2016, Vladimir_2016}.

In this study, we theoretically investigate a collinear antiferromagnetic model on a honeycomb lattice with anisotropic exchange coupling \cite{Lee_2024, Hariki_2024, Lovesey_2023}. To break time-reversal symmetry for magnon hopping between intra-sublattice sites, we incorporate complex phase factors into next-nearest-neighbor (NNN) exchange coupling—analogous to the Haldane model for electronic systems \cite{Haldane_1998}. Using linear spin wave theory \cite{Kubo_1952}, we obtain the magnon spectrum and reveal the emergence of spin-split magnon bands corresponding to two magnon modes—a defining feature of altermagnetic systems. 

Next, we employ this model to investigate magnon-mediated spin current under a thermal gradient using a density matrix-based quantum kinetic framework \cite{Harsh_2023, Harsh_2023_2, Harsh_2024, Sayan_2025, Sayan_2024}, with particular focus on the spin Nernst effect (SNE), wherein a transverse spin current is generated in response to a temperature gradient. Our results show that the anisotropic complex exchange hopping gives rise to an intrinsic contribution to the SNE, directly linked to the Berry curvature of the magnon bands, Fig. (\ref{magnon-bands}\textcolor{blue}{e}). Notably, this intrinsic response vanishes in the absence of the phase factor [see Fig. (\ref{ReJ1_ImJ1_parameter_space}\textcolor{blue}{c})], underscoring the topological nature of the effect \cite{Cui_2023}. In addition to the intrinsic mechanism, we also identify a scattering time-dependent contribution, which arises from asymmetric magnon population in the presence of thermal driving and magnon relaxation. Remarkably, this extrinsic SNE contribution is found to be two orders of magnitude larger than the intrinsic one, consistent with previous findings \cite{Cheng_2016, Park_2020, Go_2022}. 

Moreover, we analyze the magnon-mediated altermagnetic spin-splitting effect (MASSE), which is not well understood for magnon-mediated transport~\cite{Jakub_2021, Yuan_2020, Karube_2022, Giil_2024, Liao}. Our result shows that the maximum spin-splitting angle for our model is about \(16^\circ\). We also identified the criteria for generating spin current in longitudinal direction and pure spin current in transverse directions. Our study thus positions altermagnets as promising candidates for next-generation spintronic devices, enabling tunable and efficient spin transport without charge motion or reliance on weak spin–orbit interactions.


