\begin{abstract}
This study presents an NNTile framework for training large deep neural networks in heterogeneous clusters. The NNTile is based on a StarPU library, which implements task-based parallelism and schedules all provided tasks onto all available processing units (CPUs and GPUs). 
It means that a particular operation, necessary to train a large neural network, can be performed on any of the CPU cores or GPU devices, depending on automatic scheduling decisions.
Such an approach shifts the burden of deciding where to compute and when to communicate from a human being to an automatic decision maker, whether a simple greedy heuristic or a complex AI-based software.
The performance of the presented tool for training large language models is demonstrated in extensive numerical experiments.
\end{abstract}

