For the general case of arbitrary $m \ge 3,$  we can prove the assertions of the table, but are unable to prove that the lengths enumerated in the table are the only maximal run lengths. We therefore leave this as an open conjecture, noting, that the conjecture is strongly supported by the fact that it is true for many initial $m.$

\begin{conjecture} For each $m \ge 3,$ the enumerated lengths in Theorem \ref{the:runs} are in fact the only maximal run lengths.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{proof}
We suffice using the three step method indicated in Section \ref{sec:proofmethods}, with proving one component of the theorem, in this case Theorem \ref{the:runs}(f), the proof of the other cases being similar
and hence omitted. We therefore suppose given integers $m,i, 1  \le i \le m-2.$ 

\textbf{Step I:}Let $b=(2^i-1)2^m$ and let $l= 2^m-2^i.$ To prove assertion (f) it suffices to prove the following assertions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $s_m[b-1]=1$
\item $s_m[b]=0$
\item $s_m[b+j] = 0, 1 \le j <   l$    
\item $s_m[b+l] = 1.$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step II:} Using the Dictionary Lemma we calculate
\begin{itemize}
\item $(b-1)_2= 1^{i-1} \cdot 0 \cdot 1^m.$
\item $b_2 = 1^i \cdot 0^m.$
\item $(b+j)_2 = 1^i \cdot j', \text{ where } |j'| =|j_2| \text{ and } j'_v = j, 1 \le j \le   l-1$ ($j'$ equals $j_2$ left-padded with zeroes)    
\item $(b+l)_2 = 1^m \cdot 0^i.$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Step III:} By inspection and the Suffix lemma the assertions in Step I follow from the binary representations in Step II.
\end{proof}

\section{Palindromes}
There are two distinct definitions of maximal palindromes, \textit{locally maximal palindromes} which for a given sequence,$ t$, 
asserts that if for some beginning position, $b>0,$ and length $l,$ that  $t[b \dotsc b+l-1]$ is a palindrome then $t[b-1 \dotsc b+l+1]$ is not, and 
\textit{globally maximal palilndromes}, which for a given sequence, $t$ asserts that if $y=t[b \cdots b+l-1]$ is a palindrome then 
$aya$ is not a factor of $t$ for any letter $a.$  For Thue-Morse the maximal palindrome lengths are $(3 \cdot 4^n)_{n \ge 0}$. For Rudin Shapiro the maximal palindrome lengths are
$7, 10, 14,$ see (\cite[Section 8.6.4]{Shallit} who describes maximal local and global palindromes differently).   

We shall study the $n$-th \textit{largest maximal palindrome lengths}, for $n=1,2,3,4,5.$ For a given sequence, a length $l$ is an $n$-th largest maximal palindrome length if  $l$
 is a maximal global palindrome length and there are only $n-1$ distinct lengths, $l'>l$ for which there are maximal global palindromes of length $l'.$ 

It is easy with Walnut to completely discover and prove the global maximal palindrome lengths for any particular $s_m.$ To prove a general theorem, we resort to the same technique used for runs: By giving more detail, not only about the lengths, but also about starting positions, and the underlying  palindrome patterns, we can use the proof method presented in Section \ref{sec:proofmethods} to prove theorems for all $s_m.$ 

In exploring patterns, Walnut was used to discover the maximal palindrome lengths and appropriate starting positions, while Mathematica was used to discover the underlying palindrome patterns. It was hoped that some theme would emerge for the $n$-th largest palindrome length, $n=1,2,3,4,5,$ however, the results appear quite different and distinct. They are summarized in the following theorem.

