	\begin{abstract}
    We study scattering by metamaterials with negative indices of refraction, which are known to support \emph{surface plasmons} -- long-lived states that are highly localized at the boundary of the cavity. This type of states has found uses in a variety of modern technologies. In this article, we study surface plasmons in the setting of non-trapping cavities; i.e. when all billiard trajectories outside the cavity escape to infinity. We characterize the indices of refraction which support surface plasmons, show that the corresponding resonances lie super-polynomially close to the real axis, describe the localization properties of the corresponding resonant states, and give an asymptotic formula for their number. 

         % We study surface plasmons in scattering by negative index of refraction metamaterials. Lax-Phillips conjecture asks the existence of a sequence of resonances that is close to the real axis. It is known that for non-trapping obstacles with Dirichlet or Neumann boundary condition, Lax-Phillips conjecture does not hold. The scattering problem of metamaterials is reformulated as an obstacle scattering problem with pseudo-differential boundary conditions in this paper, we give boundary criteria when the Lax–Phillips conjecture holds and when it does not for non-trapping obstacles. The validity of Lax–Phillips conjecture in our setting is equivalent to the existence of surface plasmons. As in the recent work of Carvalho and Moitier, we describe these localized states as resonances of the operator $-\div (A\nabla)$ where $A$ is positive definite in the exterior of a domain $\Omega_{\inner}\Subset \mathbb{R}^d$ and negative definite in $\Omega_{\inner}$. In the case when $\Omega_{\inner}$ is non-trapping we show that the jump in $A$ determines whether resonances near the real axis, and hence surface plasmons exist. In the case where they are present, we show that they must be super-polynomially close to the real axis and prove a Weyl law for their number.
	\end{abstract}
