\section{Flexible polyhedral nets} \label{sec:flexible}
%------------------------------------------------------------------
%------------------------------------------------------------------

The first application of our method is the construction of quad-mesh mechanisms, also known as %a discrete analog of flexible surfaces, namely, 
flexible nets. We are mostly interested in flexible polyhedral %\BW{polygonal?} 
nets, i.e. flexible Q-nets.
%
We build on the classification of flexible Q-nets in isotropic geometry from \cite{pirahmad2025} (see Figs.~\ref{fig:flexible-isotropic-i} and~\ref{fig:flexible-isotropic-ii}). Let us briefly state %introduce 
this classification. 
%\BW{All the vectors and matrices should use a bold font. }
%\mscomm{Definitions and the statement of the classification theorem to be inserted here, or maybe avoid the statement to save space.}

First, we need to define what \emph{is} a flexible net in isotropic geometry. Even this step is non-trivial and has only recently been done \cite{isometric-isotropic}.  Only requiring rigid faces (\emph{face condition}) and rotational joints in the edges is not enough in isotropic geometry; otherwise, \emph{any} net would be flexible. 
The insight %for the right definition 
comes from the famous Gauss Theorema Egregium, saying that flexion of smooth surfaces preserves the Gaussian curvature. So it is natural to additionally require preservation of %discrete isotropic Gaussian 
curvature at the vertices (\emph{vertex condition}).
%This vertex condition is automatic in Euclidean geometry, but necessary in isotropic one.

%Here the discrete isotropic Gaussian curvature 
This curvature is defined as follows. 
%By an $m\times n$ \emph{net} we mean a collection of $(m+1)(n+1)$ points $f_{ij}\in I^3$ indexed by two integers $0\le i\le m$ and $0\le j\le n$, %(i.e., a map $\{0,\dots,m\}\times \{0,\dots,n\}\to I^3$), 
%such that $f_{ij},F_{i+1,j},F_{i+1,j+1},F_{i,j+1}$ are consecutive vertices of a convex quadrilateral for all $0\le i< m$ and $0\le j< n$. \mscomm{Unify the notation!}
%%Recall that a Q-net is a map $F\colon \mathbb{Z}^2\to\mthbb{R}^3$, where we denote $F(i,j)=:f_{ij}$. \mscomm{Unify the notation!}
For an $m\times n$ Q-net $f_{ij}$, denote by $p_{ij}$ the plane of the face $f_{ij}f_{i+1,j}f_{i+1,j+1}f_{i,j+1}$. Consider the planes $p_1:=p_{i-1,j-1},p_2:=p_{i,j-1},p_3:=p_{ij},p_4:=p_{i-1,j}$ of consecutive faces around a particular non-boundary vertex $f_{ij}$ (see Fig.~\ref{figure:isotopic-gaussian-curvature}(a)). The top views of the points metric dual to those planes %of these faces 
form a quadrilateral 
$\overline{p_1^*}\,\overline{p_2^*}\,\overline{p_3^*}\,\overline{p_4^*}$ called the \emph{top view of the %isotropic Gaussian image of 
face metric dual to the vertex $f_{ij}$} (see Fig.~\ref{figure:isotopic-gaussian-curvature}(b)). The oriented area of this quadrilateral is called the \emph{discrete isotropic Gaussian curvature}, % concentrated at the vertex} $f_{ij}$, 
or simply the \emph{curvature at} $f_{ij}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq-def-curvature}
\Omega(f_{ij}):= \mathrm{Area}\left(\overline{p_1^*}\,\overline{p_2^*}\,\overline{p_3^*}\,\overline{p_4^*}\right)
=\frac{1}{2} \sum_{j=1}^{4} \det\left(\overline{p_j^*}, \overline{p_{j+1}^*}\right),
%\qquad\text{where $
\quad\overline{p_{5}^*}:=\overline{p_{1}^*}. %$}.
\end{equation}
For instance, if %one of the parameter lines 
the $i$-line through $f_{ij}$ is straight (and does not contain $f_{i\pm 1,j}$; see Fig.~\ref{fig:flexible-class-i-def}(a)), then 
$\overline{p_{1}^*}=\overline{p_{4}^*}$, $\overline{p_{2}^*}=\overline{p_{3}^*}$, and hence $\Omega(f_{ij})=0$. %the curvature at $f_{ij}$ vanishes.



%Let us define the flexibility in $I^3$. It requires the usual \emph{face conditions} meaning the rigidity of faces. Compared to Euclidean geometry, we impose additional \emph{vertex conditions}, which are automatic in Euclidean geometry but become crucial in isotropic one.

An \emph{isotropic isometric deformation} of an $m\times n$ Q-net $f_{ij}$ is a continuous family of $m\times n$ Q-nets $f_{ij}(t)$, where $t\in [0,1]$
%$t\in (-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)$ for some $\varepsilon>0$ 
and $f_{ij}(0)=f_{ij}$, satisfying 
\begin{description}
    \item[face condition:] corresponding faces of all $m\times n$ Q-nets $f_{ij}(t)$ are isotropically congruent; and    
    \item[vertex condition:] corresponding non-boundary vertices of all $m\times n$ 
    Q-nets $f_{ij}(t)$ have the same curvatures. %isotropic Gaussian curvature.
\end{description}
An isotropic isometric deformation is \emph{trivial} if for each 
$t\in [0,1]$
%$t\in (-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)$ 
there is an isotropic congruence $C_t$ such that $f_{ij}(t)=C_t(f_{ij})$ for each $0\leq i\leq m,0\leq j\leq n$. 
The %$m\times n$ 
Q-net $f_{ij}$ is an \emph{isotropic flexible net} %in $I^3$ 
if it has a nontrivial isotropic isometric deformation; see Fig.~\ref{fig:flexible-class-i-def}(a).

%\mscomm{A simple example of $2\times 2$ square net here.}

All isotropic flexible nets were classified in the series of papers \cite{pirahmad2024area,pirahmad2025}. Namely, under minor convexity assumptions, %the following was proved. 
%%Under minor convexity assumptions, in a series of papers \cite{pirahmad2024area,pirahmad2025} it was shown that 
%%there are exactly two classes of isotropic flexible $m\times n$ nets (See Fig.~\ref{fig:flexible-class-i-def}):
%Consider 
an $m\times n$ Q-net %with face planes $p_{kl}$, where $0\leq k< m,0\leq l< n$. Then the net 
is an isotropic flexible net if and only if %at least 
one of the following conditions holds: % (See Fig.~\ref{fig:flexible-class-i-def}):  
    \begin{enumerate}
    %\begin{description}
    \item[\textup{(i)}] 
