Consider a discrete surface $f_{ij}$ of size \bluer{$n \times n$}. The discrete parameter lines $i = \mathrm{const}$ and $j = \mathrm{const}$  together with the diagonal polylines  joining the points $f_{ij}$ with $i - j = \mathrm{const}$ (\emph{discrete $(i - j)$-lines}), is called a \emph{discrete $3$-web}. By further extending the discrete $3$-web with the other diagonal polylines joining the points $f_{ij}$ with $i + j = \mathrm{const}$ (\emph{discrete $(i + j)$-lines}), one obtains a \emph{discrete $4$-web} (see the inset). Here all the four constants as well as $i$ and $j$ are integers. The $3$- or $4$-webs are still denoted by $f_{ij}$.

