It is striking that there is a simple approximate analytic expression for \blueit{such} \emph{isotropic CPRC surfaces} when the angle $\gamma$ is not too far from $90^\circ$ (nothing like that is available in the Euclidean case). %a right angle 


The formula is especially elegant if one uses the complex coordinate $w=x+iy$ in the $xy$-plane. Using the complex derivatives $f_w:=\frac{1}{2}\left(f_x-if_y\right)$ and $f_{\bar w}:=\frac{1}{2}\left(f_x+if_y\right)$, we %get the expressions for 
write the isotropic mean and Gaussian curvatures of a surface $z=f(w)$
as $H=2f_{w\bar w}$ and $K=4f_{w\bar w}^2-4f_{ww}f_{\bar w\bar w}$. The equation $H^2/K=-\cot^2\gamma=\mathrm{const}$ takes the form (up to an overall sign)
\begin{equation}\label{eq-complex-crpc}
f_{w\bar w}=\cos\gamma \cdot\sqrt{f_{ww}f_{\bar w\bar w}}.
%(f_{w\bar w})^2=\cos^2\alpha \cdot f_{ww}f_{\bar w\bar w}.
%f^t_{w\bar w}=\sqrt{\frac{t}{1-t}} \sqrt{f^t_{ww}f^t_{\bar w\bar w}}, \qquad t\in [0;\varepsilon].
\end{equation}
