Let us search for a solution in the form of %a power 
series in the small parameter $\varepsilon=\cos\gamma$:
%\sqrt{t/(t-1)}= $ \mscomm{insert an expression in $\alpha$}:  %by expanding it in powers of $t$: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq-taylor}
f(w)=f^{(0)}(w)+ f^{(1)}(w)\varepsilon+ f^{(2)}(w)\varepsilon^2/2+\dots,
\end{equation}
for some unknown functions $f^{(0)}(w),f^{(1)}(w),%f^{(2)}(w),
\dots$. % not depending on $t$.
Here $f^{(0)}(w)$ satisfies~\eqref{eq-complex-crpc} for %$\gamma=\pi/2$
$\varepsilon=\cos\gamma=0$, hence is a harmonic function.
Any harmonic function (in a simply-connected domain) can be written as
\begin{equation}\label{eq-f0}
    f^{(0)}(w)=2\mathrm{Re}\,g(w)
\end{equation}
for some complex analytic function $g(w)$ (roughly, a polynomial or %infinite 
power series in %the complex variable 
$w$). For an % complex 
analytic function, %we have 
$g_{\bar w}(w)=0$ and we denote $g_{w}(w)=:g'(w)$. The next function $f^{(1)}(w)$ satisfies the equation
$$
f^{(1)}_{w\bar w}=\sqrt{f^{(0)}_{ww}f^{(0)}_{\bar w\bar w}}.
%(f^{(1)}_{w\bar w})^2=f^{(0)}_{ww}f^{(0)}_{\bar w\bar w},
$$
The rhs equals $|g''(w)|$,
and it is not hard to guess a particular solution
\begin{equation}\label{eq-f1}
f^{(1)}(w)=|h(w)|^2, \quad\text{ where }h(w):=\int\sqrt{g''(w)}\,dw.
\end{equation}
Technically, we need some assumptions to get a continuous branch of the square root here; %$\sqrt{g''(w)}$; 
e.g., %we should 
%assume that 
%$g''(w)\ne 0$ in the domain or 
the requirement that all zeroes of $g''(w)$, if any, have multiplicity $2$. We present the details (unessential for implementation) in a subsequent publication. 

