For design, it is convenient to prescribe a polynomial $h'(w)$ first, then compute $h(w)$ and $g(w)$ by integration, and use~\eqref{eq-f0}--\eqref{eq-f2} to get $$f^{\mathrm{appr}}(w)=f^{(0)}(w)+f^{(1)}(w)\varepsilon +f^{(2)}(w)\varepsilon^2 /2.$$ The roots of $h'(w)$ correspond to the roots of $g''(w)$, $K$, and $H$, hence to \emph{flat points}, where more than two asymptotic lines can meet. At these points, the angle between the asymptotic curves can be different from $\gamma$, and we need to regularize $f^{(2)}(w)$, e.g., %by introducing a small parameter $\Delta>0$ and
replacing $\log|h'(w)|$ with $\log(|h'(w)|+\varepsilon)$ in~\eqref{eq-f2}. Then $z=f^{\mathrm{appr}}(w)$ is the desired approximate isotropic CPRC surface.

