\textbf{Main Objective.} 
The non-linear least-squares problem for a given \(\varepsilon\) aims to minimize  
\begin{equation}\label{eq:epsenergy}
E^{\varepsilon} = E_{hard} + \omega_{0}E_{f} + \omega_{1}E_{scl} + \omega_{2}E_{vcl},
\end{equation}  
where \(E_{hard} \in \{E^{\varepsilon}_{GGG}, E^{\varepsilon}_{AGAG}, E^{\varepsilon}_{AAG}, E_{CRPC}\}\) represents hard constraints. The energy \(E_{f}\) ensures smoothness, while $E_{scl}$ and $E_{vcl}$ prevent drastic shape changes during the optimization (here $E_{vcl}$ is the sum over all vertices $f_{ij}$).
%. The term $E_{cl}$ is the surface closeness (\(E_{cl}\)) energy. The $E_c$ is closeness to vertices energy for all vertices of the shape.  
The weights \(\omega_{0}, \omega_1\), and \(\omega_{2}\) balance fairness and approximation.   



